How maul runs it: {{if eq .Form "http"}}each step sends its request to the named component of the running preview,
in order, and fails the check unless the response matches its `expect`; `capture` keeps a value from a response for
later steps.{{else if eq .Form "browser"}}the steps run in order in a fresh browser on the running preview, each an
action or an assertion; `visible` means a person can see the element, `legible` that a person can read its text, and
`clickable` that it, not something covering it, receives a click.{{else}}the program runs without a shell in a
sandbox that can reach the running preview, and the check fails unless its exit code and output match its
`expect`.{{end}}
Every `{{"{{"}}run{{"}}"}}` is replaced by a fresh token on every run, so the data a check creates is its own.

## How to judge
{{- if .ExpectFail}}

This check failed on the build under review: maul ran it itself, each time in a fresh browser or session with fresh
data, and it failed each time at step {{.FailedStep}}, on an assertion. The other agent says that failure shows a defect:
the outcome does not hold. maul will report the outcome failed, and start a fix, only if you answer `proves`, so a
check that is wrong here makes another agent change code that works. Answer `proves` only if all three hold:

1. The check encodes the outcome as approved: if it passed, that would show the outcome holds. It does what the
   outcome describes, on data it created itself.
2. It would pass on a build where the outcome holds: nothing it expects goes beyond what the outcome states (an exact
   value, a wording, an order, a layout or a timing the outcome does not promise), and it depends on no data it did
   not create.
3. Step {{.FailedStep}}, the one that failed, asserts the outcome itself, not something incidental to it: not setup,
   navigation or a value the outcome says nothing about.

Answer `does_not_prove` if any of them does not hold, and `unclear` if you cannot tell from what is shown.
{{- else}}

Answer `proves` only if a pass of this check really shows the outcome holds: it does what the outcome describes, on
data it created itself, and it would fail if the outcome did not hold. Answer `does_not_prove` if it could pass while
the outcome does not hold: it checks something else or only part of the outcome, it asserts only that a call succeeded
or a page loaded, it is keyed to fixed values that an implementation could special-case, or it depends on data it did
not create. Answer `unclear` if you cannot tell from what is shown.
{{- end}}

You need no tool but Write: do not try to run, fetch or read anything. This review may take at most
{{.Limits.MaxTurns}} agent turns, {{.Limits.TokenBudget}} output tokens and {{.Limits.WallClock}}.

